    <li>407 J/&deg;C; 97.3 cal &deg;C<sup>&minus;1</sup></li>
</ol>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/05.E:_Thermochemistry_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_10"><span id="Q5.1.6"></span><h3 id="Q5.1.6-42016">Q5.1.6</h3>

<p>How much heat, in joules and in calories, must be added to a 75.0&ndash;g iron block with a specific heat of 0.449 J/g &deg;C to increase its temperature from 25 &deg;C to its melting temperature of 1535 &deg;C?</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Exercises:_General_Chemistry/Exercises:_OpenStax/05.E:_Thermochemistry_(Exercises)" class="mt-section" id="section_11"><span id="S5.1.6"></span><h3 id="S5.1.6-42016">S5.1.6</h3>

<p>\(q=mC&Delta;&deg;T\)</p>

<p>\(q=(75.0g)\times(\dfrac{0.449\:J}{g\:&deg;C})\times(1,510&deg;K) = 50,800J\)</p>

